 will send it out, declares the LORD of Hosts, and it will enter the house of the thief and the house of him who swears falsely by My name. It will remain inside his house and destroy it, down to its timbers and stones.”","verse":4}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.1.7-6.15","r":"Zech.1.7-6.15","rawR":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.1.7-6.15","value":"Zech.1.7-6.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:7–6:15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," These eight visions addressed the Judeans’ fears that prevented them from responding in faith to God’s promises. These visions, which occurred two months after Haggai’s final two messages (",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.10-19","r":"Hagg.2.10-19","rawR":"Hagg.2.10-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.10-19","value":"Hagg.2.10-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hag 2:10-19"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.20-23","r":"Hagg.2.20-23","rawR":"Hagg.2.20-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.20-23","value":"Hagg.2.20-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"20-23"},"), suggest that Haggai’s prophecies were being fulfilled."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.1.7-6.15","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":6,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.1.7-6.15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ebdf3bb0f9a43a2f6405ddb69741057dd776f10a855e0f493173d69a8995941c","sourceItemName":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11505,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.1.7-6.15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.1-4","r":"Zech.5.1-4","rawR":"Zech.5.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.1-4","value":"Zech.5.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1-4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Zechariah’s vision of the flying scroll reminded the leaders and people of postexilic Judah that they were still obligated to follow God’s commands. The blessings and curses of the law (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.28.1-68","r":"Deut.28.1-68","rawR":"Deut.28.1-68","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.28.1-68","value":"Deut.28.1-68"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 28"},") were still in effect."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.1-4","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.1-4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a587a4473f0e19a2bb36df08e1d19dc53070867571a0762aff637db2ef3c01f4","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.1-4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11541,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.1-4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.1-4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.1","r":"Zech.5.1","rawR":"Zech.5.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.1","value":"Zech.5.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["looked up again:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This is the sixth of eight visions that Zechariah had in the same night. • A ",{"c":["scroll"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," the equivalent of a book in biblical times, was usually made of rolled parchment or leather, but was sometimes made of papyrus, tin, or copper. This scroll was ",{"c":["flying"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," unfurled like a banner for all to see."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.1","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"922f47a071c850c230357926a4f8b79b7389df95d2562d57287670661263f735","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11542,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.3","r":"Zech.5.3","rawR":"Zech.5.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.3","value":"Zech.5.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["curse"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (or ",{"c":["oath"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},"): A covenant included curses on violators of the agreement (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.29.12-21","r":"Deut.29.12-21","rawR":"Deut.29.12-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.29.12-21","value":"Deut.29.12-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 29:12-21"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.3","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"cfff5ea9031133e74de542507abb20a9d495da5c77686974c648c37ff48a2fbe","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11543,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.4","r":"Zech.5.4","rawR":"Zech.5.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.4","value":"Zech.5.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["This curse"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is sent like a law officer to punish violations of God’s covenant (see ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.147.15","r":"Ps.147.15","rawR":"Ps.147.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.147.15","value":"Ps.147.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 147:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.55.11","r":"Isa.55.11","rawR":"Isa.55.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.55.11","value":"Isa.55.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 55:11"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.4","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d8735ffac973892282f1700e972b7e1d727570280f19092bbb769f832e34954e","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11544,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.5-11","r":"Zech.5.5-11","rawR":"Zech.5.5-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.5-11","value":"Zech.5.5-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:5-11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The seventh vision continues the theme of cleansing that began with the acquittal of the high priest Jeshua (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.3.1-10","r":"Zech.3.1-10","rawR":"Zech.3.1-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.3.1-10","value":"Zech.3.1-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1-10"},"). The removal of wickedness, much like the removal of Jeshua’s filthy garments (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.3.4","r":"Zech.3.4","rawR":"Zech.3.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.3.4","value":"Zech.3.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:4"},"), was a gracious act of pardon by the covenant-keeping God."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.5-11","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.5-11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"66967e57bd502e0d14e88836616db867a50fcd454c29f3d68e0bd9f1f2e0c1e1","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.5-11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11545,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.5-11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.5-11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.6","r":"Zech.5.6","rawR":"Zech.5.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.6","value":"Zech.5.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["basket"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["ephah"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") was a standard unit of dry measure, approximately ½ bushel. The Old Testament prophets’ condemnation of unjust ephahs creates a natural association between the ephah basket and evil (see ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.45.10","r":"Ezek.45.10","rawR":"Ezek.45.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.45.10","value":"Ezek.45.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 45:10"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mic.6.10","r":"Mic.6.10","rawR":"Mic.6.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.6.10","value":"Mic.6.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mic 6:10"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.6","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"90cdf1b94b77a2ab9a24d04dcea37ed6ad4c23379559dd46aa6f4594a6fe1467","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11546,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.7-8","r":"Zech.5.7-8","rawR":"Zech.5.7-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.7-8","value":"Zech.5.7-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:7-8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The evil ",{"c":["woman"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in a ",{"c":["basket"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," represents a seductive and dangerous force that is difficult to contain. The sin of idolatry had previously provoked God’s judgment (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.17.16-18","r":"2Kgs.17.16-18","rawR":"2Kgs.17.16-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.17.16-18","value":"2Kgs.17.16-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 17:16-18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.36.14","r":"2Chr.36.14","rawR":"2Chr.36.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.36.14","value":"2Chr.36.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 36:14"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.7-8","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.7-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"2229ab9028c10e588a35df95a14737909bd52a0c29dc0e92a92e21632a757c8c","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.7-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11547,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.7-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.7-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.7","r":"Zech.5.7","rawR":"Zech.5.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.7","value":"Zech.5.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["heavy lead cover"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["a talent"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," [seventy-five pounds] ",{"c":["of lead"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") is not a natural cover for an ephah basket, but shows that extraordinary measures were needed to seal the basket’s unholy contents."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.7","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6c89115cca682ae3dd80af4b63d24ae31c9ea24f6d995a0f7bf926afed12f266","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11548,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.8","r":"Zech.5.8","rawR":"Zech.5.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.8","value":"Zech.5.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Wickedness"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," refers to evil generally; whether moral or ceremonial, it is opposed to righteousness (see ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.13.6","r":"Pr.13.6","rawR":"Pr.13.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.13.6","value":"Pr.13.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 13:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.33.12","r":"Ezek.33.12","rawR":"Ezek.33.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.33.12","value":"Ezek.33.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 33:12"},"). The word ",{"c":["wickedness"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["rish‘ah"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") is similar in Hebrew to the name Asherah, the fertility goddess of the ancient Near East (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.7.5","r":"Deut.7.5","rawR":"Deut.7.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.7.5","value":"Deut.7.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 7:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.16.21","r":"Deut.16.21","rawR":"Deut.16.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.16.21","value":"Deut.16.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"16:21"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.8","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"aa46d5fedeacdae39875c3eb809b876ae5ef5e4c853490a4ae9910121c7ee06f","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11549,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.9","r":"Zech.5.9","rawR":"Zech.5.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.9","value":"Zech.5.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The depiction of divine or angelic winged creatures as women is unusual in the Old Testament. If the ",{"c":["two women"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," are the Lord’s servants, they are unique angels. If they are the attendants of Wickedness (a foreign goddess), their submission to God’s command demonstrates his power over false gods."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.9","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"17dbffaf533f3f6e121e7fe70aedffec677e54b99d0e21535e3aa4c52260727f","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11550,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.5.11","r":"Zech.5.11","rawR":"Zech.5.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.5.11","value":"Zech.5.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Idolatry is potently and aggressively evil; it cannot be confined, but must be shipped back to its source (Babylonia) by God’s decree. This symbolism indicates that God is able to purge his people of all the various forms of wickedness that separated them from him. • ",{"c":["The land of Babylonia"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was the land of Hebrew captivity (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.4.10","r":"Mic.4.10","rawR":"Mic.4.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.4.10","value":"Mic.4.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mic 4:10"},"). The prophets condemn it as wicked and idolatrous (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.46.1-47.15","r":"Isa.46.1-47.15","rawR":"Isa.46.1-47.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.46.1-47.15","value":"Isa.46.1-47.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 46–47"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.50.1-51.64","r":"Jer.50.1-51.64","rawR":"Jer.50.1-51.64","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.50.1-51.64","value":"Jer.50.1-51.64"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 50–51"},"). In the New Testament, Babylon represents the evil Roman Empire (",{"href":"?bref=Rev.17.5","r":"Rev.17.5","rawR":"Rev.17.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.17.5","value":"Rev.17.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rev 17:5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rev.18.2","r":"Rev.18.2","rawR":"Rev.18.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.18.2","value":"Rev.18.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:2"},"; see ",{"href":"?bref=1Pet.5.13","r":"1Pet.5.13","rawR":"1Pet.5.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Pet.5.13","value":"1Pet.5.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Pet 5:13"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.5.11","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":5,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.5.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e8a29f0f761e9a6b7444f44597b933a99c2d40d876ea46dd142159f131940302","sourceItemName":"Zech.5.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11551,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.5.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.5.11","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}